            "Transient loss of consciousness has three main causes: [[syncope|syncope]] (global cerebral hypoperfusion), epileptic seizures, and functional seizures. Jerking does not settle the question: brief jerks are common when the brain is starved of blood, a picture called [[convulsive-syncope|convulsive syncope]]. The patient remembers little of the event itself, so a witness account is the most valuable piece of evidence. A phone video, when there is one, is better still.\n\nAsk about three phases.\n\nBefore. Syncope usually has a trigger and a warning: prolonged standing, a hot crowded room, pain, the sight of blood, or standing up quickly, followed by [[presyncope|presyncope]] (lightheadedness, sweating, nausea, vision greying or tunneling). An epileptic seizure often has no warning, or a brief stereotyped [[focal-seizure|focal]] onset such as a rising epigastric feeling or deja vu.\n\nDuring. In convulsive syncope the patient goes limp and pale, and any jerks are brief, few, and irregular, starting after the fall. In a generalized [[tonic-clonic-seizure|tonic-clonic seizure]] the body stiffens, then jerks rhythmically and in step for a minute or two, often with cyanosis and noisy breathing.\n\nAfter. After syncope the patient is oriented within seconds to a minute or so, though tired. After a tonic-clonic seizure there is usually [[postictal|postictal]] confusion lasting many minutes, often with headache and aching muscles. A bite on the side of the tongue strongly favors an epileptic seizure. Urinary incontinence happens in both and does not separate them.",
