            "For an apparent unprovoked first seizure in an adult, the AAN and AES guidance supports an EEG and brain imaging (CT or MRI) as part of the routine evaluation. MRI is preferred for finding small epileptogenic lesions; CT is useful in the emergency department to exclude an acute cause such as hemorrhage. An ECG is part of the work-up of any transient loss of consciousness, to look for an arrhythmia. An epileptiform EEG or a relevant MRI lesion raises the risk of recurrence, but a normal EEG does not exclude epilepsy, since interictal recordings are often normal.\n\nThe 2015 AAN and AES guideline advises telling adults that the risk of another seizure is greatest early, in the first 2 years (estimated at 21 to 45 percent). Factors that raise the risk include a prior brain insult, an epileptiform EEG, a significant brain imaging abnormality, and a seizure from sleep. Starting an antiseizure medication immediately probably lowers the risk of a recurrence in the next 2 years, but it is unlikely to improve the chance of long-term remission, and side effects are common though usually mild. So the decision is individual: weigh the recurrence risk, the consequences of another seizure for this person (driving, work, pregnancy plans), and the patient's preferences.\n\nSafety advice applies from the day of the seizure:\n\n- Driving: stop until allowed again. The rules and seizure-free periods depend on local law; tell the patient what applies where they live and document it.\n- Water and heights: showers rather than baths, no swimming alone, no climbing or working at heights.\n- Work and machinery: avoid dangerous machinery until the risk is clearer.\n- Triggers: regular sleep, and limit alcohol.\n- Family: what to do in a seizure (place on the side, do not put anything in the mouth, time it) and to call for help if a convulsive seizure lasts 5 minutes or more, the threshold for [[status-epilepticus|status epilepticus]].",
